  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, determine the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation ${3}^{4-x}={8}^{2x+3}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{4-x} & ={8}^{2x+3} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061({3}^{4-x}) & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({8}^{2x+3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify both sides of the equation. This gives\n\n$$\n(4-x)\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(3)=(2x+3)\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(8).\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4-x)\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(3) & =(2x+3)\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(8) \\\\ 4\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(3)-x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(3) & =2x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(8)+3\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(8) \\\\ -x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(3)-2x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(8) & =3\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(8)-4\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(3) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(3)+2x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(8) & =4\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(3)-3\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(8) \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(3)+2\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(8)) & =4\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(3)-3\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(8) \\\\ x & =\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(3)-3\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(8)}{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(3)+2\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(8)}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
